{"fields":{"advisory":{"aliases":["GHSA-hrj5-67jp-wqp9"],"categories":["denial-of-service"],"collection":"crates","cvss":null,"date":"2026-11-20","description":"In `url` 2.5.8 and earlier, `Url::origin()` handled a `blob:` URL by parsing its path as another URL and calling itself on the result. It did so regardless of the inner URL's scheme, `blob` included. Each `blob:` prefix cost one stack frame, with no limit on nesting depth. `Url::parse()` accepts a URL with 10,000 `blob:` prefixes followed by `https://example.com/` (50,020 bytes in total); calling `Url::origin()` on it overflowed the default 2 MiB stack of a spawned thread.\n\nA stack overflow aborts the whole process, and `catch_unwind()` does not catch it. One such URL crashes any program that calls `Url::origin()` on untrusted input. `url::quirks::origin()` also calls `Url::origin()`. For example, Servo's `URL.origin` DOM getter calls `url::quirks::origin()`, so a script on a page can trigger the overflow.\n\n`url` 2.5.9 follows the URL Standard: `Url::origin()` takes the origin of the inner URL only when its scheme is `http`, `https`, or `file`, returns an opaque origin otherwise, and never calls itself. A `blob:` URL with another inner scheme that previously produced a tuple origin, such as `blob:ws://...` or `blob:blob:https://...`, now returns an opaque origin.","expect-deleted":false,"id":"RUSTSEC-2026-0592","informational":null,"keywords":["stack-overflow","recursion","blob"],"license":"CC0-1.0","package":"url","references":[],"related":[],"source":null,"title":"Stack overflow in `Url::origin()` on nested `blob:` URLs","url":"https://github.com/servo/rust-url/pull/1186","withdrawn":null},"code":"vulnerability","graphs":[{"Krate":{"name":"url","version":"2.5.8"},"parents":[{"Krate":{"name":"ammonia","version":"4.2.5"},"parents":[{"Krate":{"name":"common","version":"0.1.0"},"parents":[{"Krate":{"name":"client","version":"0.1.0"},"parents":[{"Krate":{"name":"csr","version":"0.1.0"}},{"Krate":{"name":"we